															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/trinitite" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1375648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1375648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958824" alt="Trinitite" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1375648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/trinitite" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1375648); return false;" >Trinitite</a></span> <span class="odate time_1340374121 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Jun 2012 14:08</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1487036">
									

<p>Very cool. I'd never have imagined this. The potential for an alien-controlled version of The Factory to covertly make us dependent on their technology and use their economic and technical clout to control governments is&#8230;depressingly similar to the real world, and therefore plausible. And giving us all smartphones with hidden alien technology or drugs which turn us into brainwashed zombies works too.</p>
<p>An overt scenario would be easier to arrange but probably less frightening-I think public and political opinion would understand that the aliens can't be trusted, and we've got enough episodes in human history to make comparison to (smallpox blankets and so on) that I think the Foundation would be able to get people to accept that these new wonderdrugs need to be tested.</p>
<p>But if the aliens were patient this could work really well-offer effective, working immortality and food machines, raise Earth's population to 20 billion and then demand payment? We wouldn't be able to go back to where we were before without mass starvation.</p>

							</div>

								
